WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.760
Cells form the basic units of life!

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.170
We know this!

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.455
And by now we even know how the discovery

00:00:11.455 --> 00:00:13.740
of cells, as seen in the tree cork, was a

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.990
breakthrough in biological studies!

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.020
Now it’s time we have a look at how the

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.939
cells actually look under a microscope!

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:25.820
For this we select onion peel as our specimen.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.340
But before we begin with the procedure, let

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.180
us first collect the necessary requirements.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.640
We will need a microscope, a slide, cover

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.840
slip, iodine solution, water and forceps.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.710
Anything else we are left with?

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.410
Well, the most important of all is the specimen!

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.000
That is an onion peel.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.953
Now that we are all set with the equipments

00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:52.239
needed, let us begin with the amazing experiment

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.870
of observing the onion cells under the microscope!

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.752
Firstly, we take the onion peel and with the

00:00:58.752 --> 00:01:01.699
help of the forceps pull away the thin lining

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.260
from the inner surface.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.040
Before placing this on the slide, we first

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.570
add a drop of water on then place the onion

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.080
peel on it.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.260
We are NOT supposed to touch the peel and

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.240
even the slide with our fingers.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.000
Next, we add two drops of dilute iodine solution.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.200
Hold on…Do you know why is iodine solution

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.420
added to this specimen?

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.111
We do this so that we can clearly

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.080
see all the PARTS of the cell.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.380
Else it is difficult to study them as they are transparent…

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.640
It would even be difficult to differentiate

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.980
between the various parts.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.483
Now we place a cover slip in this manner and

00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:44.920
make sure that no air bubbles are formed on

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:45.920
the slide.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.200
With this our slide is ready!

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.427
This entire process of setting up the microscope

00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:55.250
along with the slide is termed ‘MOUNTING’.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.295
It is also important to see that there is

00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:59.340
enough light falling on the mirror of the

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.220
microscope.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.890
Shall we observe it now?

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.520
Here we go…

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.520
Wow!

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.369
Doesn’t it look amazing?

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:10.649
Now before studying these, tell me one thing!

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:14.569
Do plant and animal cells look the same?

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:16.849
Do they have the same organelles?

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:20.620
Or are the two types different in some ways?

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.890
Well, plant and animal cells are slightly different.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.520
When cells from a human cheek are scraped

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.700
with a sterile cotton bud and mounted, similar

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.060
but not exactly same cells are seen under

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:33.860
the microscope!

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.900
Roughly, we have these thick, distinct borders

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.600
that differentiate each cell form the other

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.420
one!

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.200
Then we have these tiny dark spots scattered all over!

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:49.530
Also, we can spot this prominent, circular

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.440
structure here which is very darkly stained.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.340
Any idea what these things are?

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.140
Well, these are the cell constituents.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.080
Let us get introduced to each in the next video!

